<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Gotland</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1361 CE (approx.) · capital Roma</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Self-governing farmers' republic on the Baltic island of Gotland, rich from trade, which kept its own law and assembly until a Danish conquest in 1361.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gotland's position in the middle of the Baltic made it a trading hub from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. Its picture stones and more than 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> hoards, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spillings-hoard">Spillings hoard</a>, show immense wealth from trade with Rus', the Islamic world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutasaga">Gutasaga</a> records an agreement under which the islanders paid a yearly tribute to the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svear">Svear</a> in return for protection.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Free farmers governed themselves through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutnalthing">Gutnalthing</a>, the island's general assembly, which met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, and wrote down their own law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutalag">Gutalag</a>. Every farmer was also a trader, and Gotlanders held privileges in Novgorod and across the Baltic. In the 12th and 13th centuries the island was covered with stone churches, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/visby">Visby</a> grew into a German-dominated Hanseatic town.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tension between rural Gotlanders and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/visby">Visby</a> led to civil war in 1288. In 1361 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a> invaded, massacring the farmers' army outside Visby. Gotland then passed among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, and became Swedish in 1645 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bromsebro-1645">Treaty of Brömsebro</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gotland.jpg" alt="Gotland" width="320" height="581"><figcaption>Image: Erik Frohne, NordNordWest (for the location map of Sweden) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sweden_Gotland_location_map_modified.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1361 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Roma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Gutnish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gutnish Republic, Gutland, Land of the Gutes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1130&amp;focus=gotland" aria-label="Gotland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5078 231 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gotland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5398 487L5381 440M5398 487L5274 535"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 440h0M5274 535h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5398 487h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5398 487h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1130&amp;focus=gotland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

